1. First understand what happens

You have:

@WebServlet("/first")
public class Hello implements Servlet

This means:

"Tomcat, whenever a request comes to /first, use the Hello Servlet."

So if your application is:

http://localhost:8080/MyApp

and you visit:

http://localhost:8080/MyApp/first

Tomcat finds:

/first
   ↓
Hello Servlet

Then the Servlet lifecycle starts.

2. Your class
public class Hello implements Servlet

Servlet is an interface.

You're saying:

My Hello class is going to behave as a Servlet.

Because you are implementing the Servlet interface, Java says:

"Okay, then you must provide all the methods required by Servlet."

That's why you have these 5 methods:

init()
service()
destroy()
getServletConfig()
getServletInfo()
3. This variable
private ServletConfig servletConfig;

For now, don't worry too much about ServletConfig.

Just understand:

This is a variable inside your Servlet that will hold configuration information provided by Tomcat.

It's private:

private ServletConfig servletConfig;

So only this class directly accesses it.

4. Then init()
@Override
public void init(ServletConfig servletConfig) throws ServletException {
    this.servletConfig = servletConfig;
    System.out.println("Hello World");
}

This is the first important method.

When Tomcat creates your Servlet, it calls:

init()
Think:
Tomcat
   ↓
Creates Hello Servlet
   ↓
Calls init()

And Tomcat gives init() a ServletConfig object:

init(ServletConfig servletConfig)

You then save that object:

this.servletConfig = servletConfig;

Remember your Java concept of this:

this.servletConfig

means:

The servletConfig variable belonging to this Hello object.

While:

servletConfig

is the parameter received by init().

So:

this.servletConfig = servletConfig;

means:

Take the ServletConfig given by Tomcat and store it inside my object.

5. Then this executes
System.out.println("Hello World");

This prints:

Hello World

in the Tomcat/server console.

It does NOT print in the browser.

So:

Tomcat console
       ↑
System.out.println()
6. Now the important part: service()
@Override
public void service(
        ServletRequest servletRequest,
        ServletResponse servletResponse
) throws ServletException, IOException {

    System.out.println("Hello World servoice");
}

This method is used to handle a request.

Suppose you visit:

http://localhost:8080/MyApp/first

The flow is:

Browser
   |
   | GET /first
   ↓
Tomcat
   |
   | finds @WebServlet("/first")
   ↓
Hello Servlet
   |
   ↓
service()

So Tomcat calls:

service(...)
7. What are these two things?
ServletRequest servletRequest
ServletResponse servletResponse

Think very simply:

ServletRequest

Information coming from the browser.

Browser
   |
   | Request
   ↓
ServletRequest

For example, later you might receive:

username = Shubham
age = 22

through the request.

ServletResponse

Information going back to the browser.

Servlet
   |
   | Response
   ↓
ServletResponse
   |
   ↓
Browser

For example:

<h1>Hello Shubham</h1>

could be sent as the response.

8. But your code isn't sending a response yet

You currently have:

System.out.println("Hello World servoice");

That means:

Browser
   ↓
Request
   ↓
Servlet
   ↓
service()
   ↓
System.out.println()
   ↓
Tomcat console

It does not mean:

Browser
   ↓
Hello World

To actually send something to the browser, you'll later use:

servletResponse

For example, you'll learn something like:

PrintWriter out = servletResponse.getWriter();
out.println("Hello World");

Then the response can go back to the browser.

9. Then destroy()
@Override
public void destroy() {
    System.out.println("Hello World destroyed");
}

This happens when Tomcat is destroying/removing the Servlet.

Think:

Servlet created
     ↓
init()
     ↓
service()
     ↓
service()
     ↓
service()
     ↓
...
     ↓
destroy()

destroy() is basically:

"I'm done with this Servlet. Do your cleanup."

Again:

System.out.println(...)

prints to the server console.

10. getServletConfig()
@Override
public ServletConfig getServletConfig() {
    return this.servletConfig;
}

Earlier you did:

this.servletConfig = servletConfig;

Now you're returning it:

return this.servletConfig;

So simply:

Tomcat gives Config
       ↓
init()
       ↓
store Config
       ↓
getServletConfig()
       ↓
return Config
11. getServletInfo()
@Override
public String getServletInfo() {
    return "shubham";
}

This just returns some information about your Servlet.

You wrote:

return "shubham";

So if something asks your Servlet for its information, it gets:

shubham

It's not important for your basic Servlet flow right now.

12. Now put the whole thing together

Your code is basically doing this:

                TOMCAT
                  |
                  | creates Servlet
                  ↓
             Hello Servlet
                  |
                  ↓
                init()
                  |
                  | stores ServletConfig
                  |
                  ↓
           Wait for requests
                  |
                  ↓
       Browser requests /first
                  |
                  ↓
               service()
                  |
                  | receives
                  | ServletRequest
                  | ServletResponse
                  |
                  ↓
          "Hello World service"
          printed in console
                  |
                  ↓
       Wait for another request
                  |
                  ↓
               service()
                  |
                  ↓
                ...
                  |
                  ↓
              destroy()